                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ExtensionToLinearOrder"} Extension to linear order
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#LocalesFromPosets"} Locales from posets – Alexandroff topology
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#cauchy_completion"} Cauchy completion
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#in_univalent_universes"} In univalent universes
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#OppositeCategory"} Finite posets and Birkhoff duality
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em partial order
            on a set is a way of ordering its elements to say that some elements precede others, but allowing for the possibility that two elements may be incomparable without being the same. This is the fundamental notion in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/order+theory"} order theory
          %p
            By regarding the ordering
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leq
            as the existence of a unique
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            (namely,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thin+categories"} thin categories
            %em order category
            associated to a partial order; see
            %a{:href => "#AsACategoryWithExtraProperties"} below
            for details.
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %h3#as_a_set_with_extra_structure As a set with extra structure
          %p
            A poset may be understood as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+structure"} extra structure
          %p
            Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %strong partial order
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is a (binary)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation"} relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leq
            with the following properties:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflexive+relation"}> reflexivity
                \:
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \leq x
                always;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transitive+relation"}> transitivity
                \: if
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi y
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi z
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \leq y \leq z
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi z
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \leq z
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antisymmetric+relation"}> antisymmetry
                \: if
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi y
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \leq y \leq x
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo =
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = y
          %p
            A
            %strong poset
            is a set equipped with a partial order.
          %h3#as_a_preorder_with_antisymmetry As a preorder with antisymmetry
          %p
            A poset is precisely a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proset"} proset
            satisfying the extra condition that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi y
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \leq y \leq x
            implies that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = y
          %h4#in_dependent_type_theory In dependent type theory
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = y
            representing equality is not necessarily valued in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-propositions"} h-propositions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+types"} equivalence of types
            between the identity type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = y
            and the condition
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi y
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \leq y \leq x
          %p
            A
            %strong partial order
            is a preorder
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            with an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+types"} equivalence of types
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} antisym
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mi X
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{antisym}(x, y):(x =_X y) \simeq (x \lt y) \times (y \lt x)
            for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:X
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y:X
          %p
            Equivalently, one could
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inductively+define"} inductively define
            a function
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} idToOrd
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mi X
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:X, y:X \vdash \mathrm{idToOrd}(x, y):(x =_X y) \to (x \lt y) \times (y \lt x)
          %p
            on reflexivity of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+type"} identity type
            by reflexivity of the preorder
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} idToSymCon
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} id
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} refl
                    %mo ≤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{idToSymCon}(x, x)(\mathrm{id}_X(x)) \coloneqq \mathrm{refl}_{\leq}(x)
          %p
            Then, a partial order is a preorder such that the defined function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} idToOrd
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{idToOrd}(x, y)
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+types"} equivalence of types
            for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:X
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y:X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} antisym
                  %mo :
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} isEquiv
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} idToOrd
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{antisym}:\prod_{x:X} \prod_{y:X} \mathrm{isEquiv}(\mathrm{idToOrd}(x, y))
          %p
            In either case, since the preorder is valued in propositions, the antisymmetry axiom ensures that the partial order is an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-set"} h-set
          %h3#AsACategoryWithExtraProperties As a category with extra properties
          %p
            A poset
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+preorders+and+%280%2C1%29-categories"} may be understood
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+property"} extra property
            = succeed "." do
              %em order category
          %p
            A
            %strong poset
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+preorders+and+%280%2C1%29-categories"} is
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            such that:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                for any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pair"} pair
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ,
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            %li
              %p
                if there is a morphism from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
                and a morphism from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
                to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                (which by the above implies that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
                are
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphic"} isomorphic
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo =
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = y
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+preorders+and+%280%2C1%29-categories"}> Equivalently
            , this says that a poset is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/skeletal+category"} skeletal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thin+category"} thin
            category, or equivalently a skeletal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+enriched"} category enriched
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian monoidal category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+values"} truth values
            or equivalently a skeletal
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%280%2C1%29-category"} (0,1)-category
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+poset"} enriched poset
          %p
            When we do this, we are soon
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+preorders+and+%280%2C1%29-categories"} led to contemplate
            a slight generalization of partial orders: namely
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preorder"} preorders
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \le y
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \le x
            imply
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = y
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
            in a certain sense. (On the other hand, it does not violate it if taken as a
            %em definition
            of
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
          %h3#monotone_functions Monotone functions
          %p
            The morphisms of partially ordered sets are
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monotone+functions"} monotone functions
            %strong monotone function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            from a poset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            to a poset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            (seen as
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structured+sets"} structured sets
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leq
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi y
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \leq y \;\Rightarrow\; f(x) \leq f(y) .
          %p
            Equivalently, it is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            (seen as certain categories).
          %p
            In this way, posets form a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pos"} Pos
          %h3#intervals Intervals
          %p
            A (closed bounded)
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval"} interval
            in a poset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is a set of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi r
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi r
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [x,y] = \{r\in C|x\le r\le y\}.
          %p
            A poset is
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+finite+poset"} locally finite
            if every closed bounded interval is finite.
          %h3#kinds_of_posets Kinds of posets
          %p
            A poset with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/top+element"} top element
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bottom+element"} bottom element
            is called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong bounded
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
            of a poset may be bounded without being a bounded as a poset in its own right.) More generally, it is
            %strong bounded above
            if it is has a top element and
            %strong bounded below
            if it has a bottom element.
          %p
            A poset with all finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/meets"} meets
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/joins"} joins
            is called a
            = succeed ";" do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice"} lattice
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semilattice"} semilattice
          %p
            A poset in which every finite set has an upper bound (but perhaps not a
            %em least
            upper bound, that is a join) is a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+set"} directed set
          %p
            As remarked above, a poset in which each interval
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [x,y]
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite set
            is called
            %strong locally finite
            or a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causet"} causet
          %p
            A poset with a bounding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+set"} countable subset
            is called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
                \-bounded
            = succeed "-bounded" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x_n)_{n=1}^{N}
            (where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            is a natural number or infinity) such that for every
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            in the poset there is an
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_m
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo ≤
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \leq x_m
            = succeed "-bounded" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi m
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_m \leq y
            instead.) Note that every bounded poset is
            = succeed "-bounded," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo =
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N = \infty
            = succeed "-bounded," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
          %h3#in_higher_category_theory In higher category theory
          %p
            A poset can be understood as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%280%2C1%29-category"} (0,1)-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertical+categorification"} vertical categorification
            of the notion of poset to that of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-poset"} n-poset
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#ExtensionToLinearOrder Extension to linear order
          #LinearExtension.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              On a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite set
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+order"} partial order
              may be
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+extension+of+a+partial+order"} extended
              to a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+order"} linear order
            %p
              For non-finite sets this still holds with the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
          %p
            See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+extension+of+a+partial+order"} linear extension of a partial order
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "linear+extension+of+a+partial+order#LinearExtensionOfAPartialOrder"} this Prop.
          %h3#LocalesFromPosets Locales from posets – Alexandroff topology
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              a poset, write
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Up
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Up(P)
              for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              whose underlying
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              is the underlying set of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              and whose
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subset"} open subset
              %em upward closed subsets
              of
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subset P
              with the property that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi and
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    ((x \in U) and (x \leq y)) \Rightarrow (y \in U)
                    \\,.
            %p
              This is called the
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexandroff+topology"} Alexandroff topology
              on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
          #UpIfFFAndPreservesLimits.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              This construction naturally extends to a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+and+faithful+functor"} full and faithful functor
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Up
                    %mo :
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Up :
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poset"} Poset
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo →
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo →
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Locale"} Locale
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              a poset, there is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+equivalence"} natural equivalence
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Up
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi P
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Sh(Up(P)) \simeq [P,Set]
            %p
              between the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sheaves"} category of sheaves
              on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale"} locale
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Up
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Up(P)
              and the category of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/copresheaves"} copresheaves
              on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
          %p
            For more see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexandroff+topology"} Alexandroff topology
          %h3#cauchy_completion Cauchy completion
          %p
            Every poset is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+complete+category"} Cauchy complete category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prosets"} prosets
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Rosolini"} Rosolini
          %h3#in_univalent_universes In univalent universes
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            with a partial order in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalent+universe"} univalent universe
            is a poset. (
            %a{:href => "#Tosun"}> Tosun
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#ANST"} Ahrens, North, Shulman, Tsementzis
          %h3#OppositeCategory Finite posets and Birkhoff duality
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi FinPoset
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FinPoset
            be the category of finite posets and order-preserving functions, and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi FinDistLat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FinDistLat
            be the category of finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+lattices"} distributive lattices
            and lattice homomorphisms. These are contravariantly equivalent, thanks to the presence of an
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ambimorphic+object"} ambimorphic object
          %p
            %strong Proposition.
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi FinPoset
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FinPoset
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalent
            to
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi FinDistLat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FinDistLat
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi FinPoset
                    %mi op
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi FinDistLat
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  FinPoset^{op} \simeq FinDistLat.
          %p One direction of this equivalence is given by the hom-functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi FinPoset
                    %mi op
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ≃
                  %mi FinDistLat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [-,2] \;\colon\; FinPoset^{op} \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} FinDistLat
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 = \{0,1\}
            is the 2-element poset with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \lt 1
            and for any
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} FinPoset
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \in \FinPoset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [Y,2]
            is the distributive lattice of poset morphisms from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi FinDistLat
                    %mi op
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ≃
                  %mi FinPoset
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [-,2] \;\colon\; FinDistLat^{op} \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} FinPoset
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            is the 2-element distributive lattice and for any
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi FinDistLat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in  FinDistLat
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X,2]
            is the poset of distributive lattice morphisms from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
          %p
            This
            %strong Birkhoff duality
            is (in one form or another) mentioned in many places; the formulation in terms of hom-functors may be found in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gavin+C.+Wraith"}> Gavin C. Wraith
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Using the generic interval
              %strong XXXIV
              4 (1993) pp.259-266. (
              %a{:href => "http://archive.numdam.org/ARCHIVE/CTGDC/CTGDC_1993__34_4/CTGDC_1993__34_4_259_0/CTGDC_1993__34_4_259_0.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p The functorial nature of the correspondence means that morphisms of finite posets (i.e. order-preserving maps) naturally correspond to morphisms of finite distributive lattices (i.e. order-preserving maps that also respect meet and join).
          %p
            It follows from Birkhoff’s representation theorem that every finite distributive lattice can be seen as a lattice of sets (i.e. sets with join and meet given by union and intersection) – in particular, sets whose elements are the join-irreducible elements of the lattice. Furthermore, a good intuition for why this duality holds is that either an element is generated as the join of existing elements, or it is join-irreducible. Hence given any existing poset, we can simply add all missing joins, and also a bottom (i.e. the nullary join). By general results (the adjoint functor theorem for posets) this suffices to ensure that all meets exist as well. This is analogous to the free colimit completion of a category, and indeed Birkhoff representation can be seen as a very special case of the Yoneda lemma as applied to (0,1)-category theory (i.e., order theory), since (0,1)-presheaves are functors into Bool rather than Set and hence correspond to
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lower+set"} lower set
          %p Birkhoff duality does not hold for infinite posets.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+algebra"} function algebra
                of
                = succeed "-valued" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+number"} rational
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
                on any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is a partial order.
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+algebra"} function algebra
                of
                = succeed "-valued" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+real+number"} Cauchy real
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is a partial order. In fact, it is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+completion"} Cauchy completion
                of the function algebra of
                = succeed "-valued" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+number"} rational
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
                on
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %li
              %p
                More generally, if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
                on the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
                with a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quadratic+form"} quadratic form
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                is a partial order, and its Cauchy completion is a vector space of same dimension on the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+real+numbers"} Cauchy real numbers
                with a quadratic form. In particular, the Cauchy
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
                and the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gaussian+rationals"} Gaussian rationals
                are partial orders.
            %li
              %p In a logic with implications, the set of propositions is partially ordered with respect to the implication as a relation.
